::ENG2_U03_L04_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "We cross this bridge to reach work. We cross this bridge to visit family. We cross this bridge to bring help when help is needed." Question: What rhetorical choice is used?{
=A. Repetition#Correct. The phrase "We cross this bridge" is repeated for emphasis.
~B. Definition#Incorrect. The passage does not define a term.
~C. Dialogue#Incorrect. No characters speak to each other.
~D. Statistics#Incorrect. No numbers or data appear.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "Shall we wait until the boards fail beneath us, or shall we act while repair is still possible?" Question: What is the effect of the rhetorical question?{
~A. It asks for a literal written answer.#Incorrect. The question is used to make a point.
=B. It pushes the audience to see immediate repair as the responsible choice.#Correct. The question makes delay seem dangerous.
~C. It changes the subject to school schedules.#Incorrect. The subject remains bridge repair.
~D. It removes urgency from the speech.#Incorrect. The question increases urgency.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Essay Passage: "A private well may serve one house, but a public well serves the whole road." Question: Which rhetorical choice shapes the sentence?{
~A. Repetition of the same phrase#Incorrect. The sentence is built on contrast, not repetition.
~B. A statistic#Incorrect. No numerical evidence is included.
=C. Contrast between private and public benefit#Correct. The sentence contrasts one house with the whole road.
~D. Dialogue between neighbors#Incorrect. No dialogue appears.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "Open the gates for the child who needs a field. Open the gates for the worker who needs shade. Open the gates for the elder who needs a quiet bench." Question: What is the effect of the repeated phrase "Open the gates"?{
~A. It makes the park seem useful to only one person.#Incorrect. The repeated examples show many people benefit.
~B. It hides the author's purpose.#Incorrect. The repetition makes the purpose clearer.
~C. It proves the park is already closed forever.#Incorrect. The author argues for opening the park.
=D. It emphasizes that different community members would benefit from access.#Correct. Repetition links several groups to the same action.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Essay Passage: "Survival is not a privilege for the fortunate; it is a duty shared by neighbors." Question: What appeal does this sentence mainly use?{
=A. An appeal to fairness and shared responsibility#Correct. The sentence argues that survival should not depend on privilege and should be a shared duty.
~B. An appeal to humor#Incorrect. The sentence is serious, not humorous.
~C. An appeal to unrelated entertainment#Incorrect. The sentence focuses on public responsibility.
~D. An appeal to silence#Incorrect. The sentence asks readers to value shared duty, not silence.
}
